From 4a2a34990dd411b1d9787c1fd7962fca7a8352df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yangsonglin13 <yangsonglin566@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 15:58:59 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] refactor(rfork): harden session-managed weight transfer
 lifecycle

Split RFork into focused config, identity, manifest, planner, seed,
session, tensor-layout, and transfer modules. Manage registered storage,
leases, heartbeat, fallback, and shutdown through RForkSession.

Derive the seed compatibility key from the built model's transferable
tensor structure and keep shard-specific identity, so replicas reuse only
layout-compatible weights. Keep runtime scratch buffers such as
topk_indices_buffer local to each instance instead of failing collection
on shape mismatch across instances with different scheduler limits.

Keep seed services alive across transient planner failures with
background retries, require a destination registration cache for reads,
restore mutable-weight safeguards, validate manifest tensor names, and
guard session shutdown against reentrant cleanup. Limit planner exception
handling to expected failures and add bounded tensor layout diagnostics.

Add seed_port_base config (default 0 = OS-assigned); each rank reserves
adjacent main/draft seed HTTP port slots and falls back to OS assignment
when a port is occupied or out of range. Initialize the YuanRong
TransferEngine with host:0 and read the bound port back via
get_rpc_port(), removing the check-then-bind race and finalizing the
engine if the read-back fails so RPC threads and ports never leak.

Document the transfer lifecycle synchronization contract, the YuanRong
Datasystem install requirement, and the RPC port range env vars, with
unit test coverage for identity validation, lease release, registration
cache isolation, restored lifecycle, and transfer timing.

Validate shared weight skips against the remote manifest only: those names
are filtered out of the local manifest by design, so requiring them in both
manifests rejected every partially shared draft model. Reject a skip that is
still present locally, which means the exclusion filter diverged from the
skip set.

